ve these different  layers graphite can actually conduct  electricity so there's actually  electrons but SUIM is these layers of  graphite that are free to move and carry  the charge so graphite conducts  electricity also because it's got these  different layers it could be used as a  pencil led because as it's pressed down  on the page layers come off onto the  page it can also be used as a lubricant  so lubricant and pencil lead now we use  it rather than the metal lead because  lead is actually poisonous now diamond  because diamond is so hard we can use it  as a cutting tool so that's giant  molecular let's move on and have a think  about simple molecular so whenever we  have molecular in these titles we know  that it's to do with covalent bonding  again sharing of electrons just like  this is covalent this is also covalent  which means also it's non metals now the  big difference between giant molecular  and simple molecular is that  giant molecular forms these repeating  units these big structures that keep on  repeating and repeating and repeating  simple molecular are much more  straightforward so we have these simple  small molecules such as Oh to co2 and  h2o or so known as water so what we can  see is they are they don't have these  big repeating units they have strong  covalent bonds in between the atoms but  apart from that let's have a look at two  water molecules even though within the  molecule we have strong covalent bonds  between the two molecules there's only  very weak attractions weak inter  molecular forces because they're really  weak intermolecular forces simple  molecular compounds have very low  melting and boiling points very low so  it doesn't take much energy to turn  these from a liquid to a gas or from a  solid to a liquid so yeah examples are  just these molecules such as Oh to co2  h2o or all examples of these simple  molecules not big repeating units also  they do not conduct electricity don't  conduct again this is because they don't  have any electrons that are free to move  so they did not conduct electricity okay  next up we are going to look at the  third type which is ionic now ionic  ionic bonding occurs between a metal and  a non metal so examples will include  things like calcium  fluoride or iron claw fluoride things  with a metal and a nonmetal a metal and  the nonmetal now the way that these work  is we have these big structures called  an ionic lattice where we have lots and  lots of ions remember'd ion is just a  Chuck and atom with a charge that's  either gained or lost electrons we have  all of these positive and negative ions  that are held in place so here is a  model of an ionic compound we have a  positive metal ions and are negative non  metal ions the reason why they're held  in place is because the strong electric  electrostatic attraction between the  positive ions and the negative ions  because remember in science opposites  attract so we can see whenever we have a  positive ion its neighbored by a  negative ion and vice-versa  as a result they have an extremely  extremely high melting and boiling point  because of this strong electrostatic  attraction between the two it takes  loads and loads of energy of heat energy  to break them apart  now another thing about a lot of ionic  compounds is they are in fact soluble  many of them are soluble which means  they dissolve in water the reason that  they are soluble is because the water  comes in and it breaks it breaks the  lattice of I breaks these ions apart so  the ions are now free to move okay when  an ionic compound is solid  it does not conduct electricity that is  because none of the ions are free to  move if we see when they're solid none  of free to move however if it's  dissolved in solution or if it's melted  and we've broken all of these ions apart  it does conduct electricity because  these ions are free to move and carry  the charge so when dissolved or molten  it does conduct electricity okay so we  just need to know about one more type of  bonding which is metallic bonding now  unfortunately I don't have a model of  metallic bonding so this only involves  metal atoms okay  so it just involves metal it does not  involve nonmetals remember these  sympathy's molecular ones and involve  nonmetals ionic convert involves metal  and the non metal metallic is just metal  now the way that this works if we have  loads and loads of these positive metal  ions and surrounding these metal ions we  have a sea of delocalized electrons so  all of the electrons from the outer  shell of these metal ions metal ions you  are free to move so they have a sea of  delocalized electrons okay because these  electrons are free to move it means that  metals do conduct electricity because if  we put a voltage through this metal  these electrons are free to move and  carry that  charge so they are free to move  therefore metals do conduct electricity  examples of metals include things like  just zinc aluminium copper these are all  examples of metals now the other thing  we need to know about metals is they  have an extremely high melting point  they have a really high melting point  because there's a large attraction  between these positive ions and these  negative electrons holding them in place  so it means we need a huge amount of  energy in order to turn these from a  solid into a liquid so they have a very  very high melting point  they are also insoluble in water so  those are the basics of our four  different types of bonding including  examples properties and models of most  their structures thank you
 

